21:40:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Plastic can be transparent because its electrons are usually bound tightly to individual molecules. Visible light does not have the right energy to excite those electrons, so much of the light can pass through. Whether a plastic is clear or opaque then often depends on its structure: - **Amorphous, uniform plastic** (such as clear acrylic or polycarbonate) scatters little light, so it looks transparent. - **Crystalline regions, fibers, fillers, bubbles, or rough surfaces** scatter light in many directions, making plastic look cloudy or opaque. Ordinary metals are different because they contain many **free, mobile electrons**. Visible light drives those electrons to move, and they rapidly re-radiate or absorb the light. This is why metals are shiny and reflective—and why a thick piece of metal does not transmit visible light. Light penetrates only a very small distance into most metals, often just tens of nanometres. So it is not strictly impossible to make a metal transmit light: - **Very thin metal films** can be partly transparent. Thin gold leaf, for example, transmits greenish light. - Fine **metal meshes** can appear transparent from a distance. - “Transparent conductors” such as indium tin oxide are used in touchscreens. They are not ordinary metals; they are wide-band-gap oxides with enough mobile electrons to conduct electricity but not enough to strongly block visible light. A bulk, solid, ordinary metal cannot generally be both highly electrically metallic and fully transparent in visible light because the same free electrons that make it metallic also strongly interact with visible light.
